SHELL=/bin/bash
PATH=/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin
TZ=Europe/Berlin
# Session diagnostics (fetch_flightstatus.py, "diagnostics"): on through this
# day, then off by themselves. Two nights and the days between, to see what the
# page that comes without a bot check is — and what a checked one looks like.
FIS_DIAG_UNTIL=2026-10-13
# The door (flightstatus/door.py, 2026-10-30): when three sessions in a row open
# without the bot check and are refused, a run waits this many minutes and tries
# one more, until the next line below is due; then it stops. A run by hand
# (docker compose exec ...) does not have this and stops at once.
FIS_DOOR_WAIT_MIN=15

# Lufthansa flight-status fetch — 15 slots/day, tiered by fleet type.
#
# Two measurements drive this layout (2026-07-26, see docs/flightstatus_schedule.md):
#
#   1. The reassignment hazard barely varies with lead time — roughly 1-1.7
#      changes per 100 leg-hours everywhere from D0 out to D+5 — so there is no
#      "decision moment" to catch. What a lookup buys is not depth but
#      resolution: how tightly we can bracket a change we already know happened.
#   2. A change first seen at D+4/D+5 is the tail that actually flies 0-5% of
#      the time. Those lookups bought almost no label value, so they were cut
#      and now fund high-cadence tracking closer in.
#
# So coverage is split into a DEEP tier (B748 + A388, ~33 numbers) tracked every
# ~2.6h at D+1/D+2, a WIDE tier (787/A350, ~78 numbers) that gets the deep
# tier's cheap extras (D+2 on sweep-lite, D+3 on sweep-full, D+4 on the far
# pass; since 2026-10-06) but no pulses and no D+5..D+9 probe — only one look at
# each flight day further out, for the timetable (see "Timetable" below) — and a
# BROAD rest (chained short-haul legs, strays) with the near window and one far
# look at D+3.
#
#   00:10 pulse        deep tier x D+1/D+2                            ~66
#   02:00 pulse                                                       ~66
#   02:45 watch 4h     red-eyes (the 03:45/05:00 departures)           ~5
#   03:30 pulse        nothing else runs 03:00-05:00, so the pulse     ~66
#                      cadence would gap here (the far pass's place
#                      until 2026-10-12, see "Far pass by day" below)
#   05:45 sweep-lite   truth D-1/D-2 + forecast D0/D1                 ~480
#                      (FIS_LOOKAHEAD_DAYS=1), + deep/wide D+2 —
#                      overnight re-plans, done before the first bank
#   08:15 census       a sixth of the long-haul numbers the catalog    ~47
#                      does not sweep, at D+8 (not one of the 15
#                      slots: it logs its own kind of run)
#   09:00 pulse                                                       ~66
#   10:30 watch 4.5h   midday bank        (deps ~11:00-15:00)         ~32
#   11:20 far pass     D+3 all; deep + wide D+4; deep D+1/D+2 — it    ~311
#                      asks the nearest days first, so it is the
#                      midday pulse with ~245 more lookups behind it
#   13:00 watch 4.5h   afternoon bank     (deps ~13:30-17:30)         ~40
#   14:10 pulse                                                       ~66
#   15:30 watch 4.5h   late-afternoon bank(deps ~16:00-20:00)         ~47
#   16:45 pulse                                                       ~66
#   18:15 sweep-full   truth + D0..D2, + deep/wide D+3 (closes the    ~620
#                      39h far->sweep seam) — D0 is queried first
#                      (priority sort), so the evening bank departs on
#                      fresh data; also sets tomorrow's plan
#   21:15 watch 4.5h   evening bank + just-after-midnight departures  ~30
#   22:00 pulse + horizon probe: deep x D+1/D+2, then D+5..D+9,      ~315
#                      then the wide tier's flight days of D+5..D+8
#                      not asked for yet (~90: the day new at D+8)
#                                                          total    ~2240/day
#
# The wide tier's extras (+~234/day) were chosen by thinning the deep tier's own
# history to the broad schedule and scoring the booking model on the same legs:
# sweep D+2/D+3 + far D+4 recover ~80% of what the full deep schedule buys
# 24-96h out; the pulses (~14 lookups per number a day) only the last ~20%
# (docs/flightstatus_schedule.md, 2026-10-06).
#
# Combined with the three sweeps, a deep-tier leg gets 10 looks/day at D+1/D+2
# with a maximum gap of ~3h — tight enough to date a reassignment to a few
# hours instead of the ~23h brackets the old two-sweep cadence produced.
#
# Sweeps refresh the whole catalog window (near: D-2..D+2, plus discovery/
# pairing/lifecycle); the far pass extends read-only to D+3 (D+4 deep); watch
# passes re-check only flights departing in the next few hours; pulse passes
# re-check only the deep tier at D+1/D+2. run_nightly.sh jitters each start
# (10 min for pulses and the far pass so the cadence holds, 15 for watches so
# the bank timing holds, 30 for the sweeps, which are not time-critical) and
# skips the slot if a previous run is still active (flock), so slots never
# overlap into doubled request rates. Every slot has >=23 min of margin to the next at worst-case
# jitter + duration; a skipped pulse just widens one bracket. When the feed
# blocks us outright, a run stops after FIS_MAX_CONSECUTIVE_BLOCKS (10) blocked
# lookups in a row instead of crawling on with backoff for hours and making the
# flock skip the next slots (lost evening/morning sweeps, 2026-09-29/10-01).
#
# When the feed is shut (2026-10-30: the timetable page comes without its bot
# check, and every lookup is refused — nights since August, from 27 Oct whole
# days), a run no longer spends ~19 min on 21 sessions and stops: after three
# such sessions it waits FIS_DOOR_WAIT_MIN (above) and tries one more, holding
# no session, until 3 min before the next line here is due. A run that waited
# stops reading then too, so it gives way to the next slot instead of making it
# skip.
#
# Far pass by day (2026-10-12): it ran at 03:30 until then, in the hours where
# lufthansa.com often serves the timetable page without its bot check — and a
# session that was not checked has every lookup refused (02:00-06:30 Berlin;
# the retry cannot help, a fresh session loads the same page). 37 of the 53 runs
# that went wrong in four weeks were in the four night slots, and from 8 Oct the
# far pass failed every night. It is the only read of D+4 for the 787/A350 and
# one of two of their D+3, so those days went missing on the pages. It now has
# the 11:35 pulse's place, and 03:30 a plain pulse in exchange: the hours around
# noon are the cleanest in the log (11h and 13h: not one day in 22 with a fifth
# of the lookups refused; 09h is clean too but leaves no room before the watch).
# It starts at 11:20 rather than 11:35, with the pulses' 10 min of jitter, to be
# done before the 13:00 watch: ~311 lookups take 55-65 min by day, so it ends
# by ~12:35 at the latest. The deep tier's afternoon bracket widens from 2.6h to
# 2.8h (3.0h at worst-case jitter); lookups per day are unchanged.
#
# Horizon probe (2026-09-24): FIS publishes a tail up to D+9 (D+10 returns no
# flight at all), and awards are booked ~1-2 weeks out, so what a D+5..D+9
# assignment is worth decides whether the booking planner can use it. The 22:00
# pulse carries those offsets for the deep tier only (~33 numbers x 5 more
# dates); nearest offsets go first, so its D+1/D+2 cadence is unchanged. It sits
# in the quietest gap of the day (next slot 00:10). The dashboard keeps capping
# upcoming flights at D+4 (BOOK_HORIZON_DAYS) until the probe has been scored
# (tools/benchmark_booking.py, its 120h..216h rows).
#
# Timetable (2026-10-13): what the booking planner lists beyond the days it has
# read is a projection of the last four weeks, which a change of timetable (the
# last Sunday of October and of March) makes wrong for weeks: flights that
# ended stay on the list, new ones are missing, and a new flight number only
# entered the catalog once it had flown. Two additions let the feed itself be
# the timetable as far as it reaches:
#   - the 22:00 pulse also asks for each wide-tier number on the days of
#     D+5..D+8 the feed has not answered for yet (FIS_TIMETABLE_OFFSETS; once
#     per flight and day, so ~90 a night, the day new at D+8; up to 160 while
#     there is catching up to do). ~55 min typical with the probe, ~110 min at
#     the slowest pace seen: done before the 00:10 pulse (a catch-up night at
#     that pace would cost that one pulse). Not D+9: at 22:00 the feed does
#     not have that day's late departures yet.
#   - the census (08:15, --census): of the long-haul block LH400-799 — 128 of
#     the 138 widebody numbers — the numbers the catalog does not sweep, a
#     sixth a day, asked for at D+8. A widebody found on one enters the catalog
#     with the next sweep, a week before the flight operates instead of after
#     it. ~47 lookups, nearly all answered "no such flight" at once; it starts
#     after the morning sweep (which ends by ~08:00) and is done by ~08:35.
#     Six days a round so the weekday asked about moves on by one each time.
# A lookup answered "no such flight" now keeps that answer (raw = '{}'), so it
# can be told from one that was refused: the timetable look does not ask twice,
# and the planner drops a projected flight the feed says is not there.
10  0 * * *  root  . /etc/environment && NIGHTLY_JITTER_MAX_S=600 /app/run_nightly.sh --pulse >> /var/log/lhlogging/flightstatus.log 2>&1
0   2 * * *  root  . /etc/environment && NIGHTLY_JITTER_MAX_S=600 /app/run_nightly.sh --pulse >> /var/log/lhlogging/flightstatus.log 2>&1
45  2 * * *  root  . /etc/environment && NIGHTLY_JITTER_MAX_S=900 /app/run_nightly.sh --watch 4 >> /var/log/lhlogging/flightstatus.log 2>&1
30  3 * * *  root  . /etc/environment && NIGHTLY_JITTER_MAX_S=600 /app/run_nightly.sh --pulse >> /var/log/lhlogging/flightstatus.log 2>&1
45  5 * * *  root  . /etc/environment && NIGHTLY_JITTER_MAX_S=1800 FIS_LOOKAHEAD_DAYS=1 /app/run_nightly.sh >> /var/log/lhlogging/flightstatus.log 2>&1
15  8 * * *  root  . /etc/environment && NIGHTLY_JITTER_MAX_S=600 /app/run_nightly.sh --census >> /var/log/lhlogging/flightstatus.log 2>&1
0   9 * * *  root  . /etc/environment && NIGHTLY_JITTER_MAX_S=600 /app/run_nightly.sh --pulse >> /var/log/lhlogging/flightstatus.log 2>&1
